The chip type is looked at before it is a family

applySetup() takes the chip type the caller asked for, or the one the bank was
made for, and casts it to OPNFamily, which has two values. The number comes from
outside both ways -- opn2_setChipType() takes any int, and a WOPN file names its
chip in a byte -- so a number that is no family is cast to one, stored, and read
back as one, which is undefined behaviour. UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer stops the
program at the read:

  src/opnmidi_opn2.cpp:568:64: runtime error: load of value 79, which is not a
  valid value for type 'OPNFamily'

The chip the library is named for stands in for a number that is no family, the
way opn2_setVolumeRangeModel() takes a number that is no model for the automatic
choice.

This came from a fuzz target of ADLplug-Next that gives the real-time API what a
host and a saved project can ask of it: the chip type comes out of a project,
which the plugin does not write itself, and out of a bank file that anyone can
hand over.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by
cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is
upstream.

diff --git a/src/opnmidi_midiplay.cpp b/src/opnmidi_midiplay.cpp
index a411d18..2a453e9 100644
--- a/src/opnmidi_midiplay.cpp
+++ b/src/opnmidi_midiplay.cpp
@@ -148,6 +148,13 @@ void OPNMIDIplay::applySetup()
     else
         chipType = m_setup.chipType;
 
+    /* The number comes from outside: opn2_setChipType() takes any int, and a bank
+       file names the chip it was made for in a byte of its own. A number that is
+       no family would be cast to one and read back as one, which is undefined, so
+       the chip the library is named for stands in for it. */
+    if(chipType < 0 || chipType >= OPNChip_Count)
+        chipType = OPNChip_OPN2;
+
     synth.reset(m_setup.emulator, m_setup.PCM_RATE, static_cast<OPNFamily>(chipType), this);
     m_chipChannels.clear();
     m_chipChannels.resize(synth.m_numChannels, OpnChannel());
